From f8bff99ec80e7cec751b9ac718d323070b114267 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 00:34:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] Tier 1: the 128-bit integers as pairs of 64-bit halves
 (#141)

ir_wide.c splits a function's I128 values into I64 halves between SSA
promotion and the optimizer (the driver's new `wide` stage), so that the
optimizer, selection, both allocations and their certificates see 64-bit
values alone. Each I128 instruction becomes its halves' instructions in
its place: additions and subtractions with their carry or borrow from an
unsigned compare; products as the low halves' product and its high half
plus the cross products not known to be zero; shifts by a constant
(through the count's conversions, as `r >> 64` comes) as shifts and an
or, a half alone, or the other half itself, and by a variable count by
its low 6 bits and selects on its bit 6; comparisons from both halves;
extensions, truncations, pointer conversions, selects, phis, loads and
stores per half. Divisions and floating conversions are libgcc calls
already; an I128 in a signature or an operation the split doesn't take
refuses the body as unsupported. Every function is scanned for an I128
instruction, 64 type bytes a compare on the vector path, one at a time
on the scalar twin; the rewrite is scalar, two passes of one body.

The IR gains `mulhu`, the high 64 bits of two I64s' unsigned product
(verifier, interpreter, parser, printer, dataflow and round-trip oracles,
tests/ir/wide.ir), and selection TIER1_RULE_MULTIPLY_HIGH: an operand in
rax, mul of the other, the result in rdx, saved around as a division by
a reciprocal is. A product of two zero extensions is imul and mul alone.

The halves found that the straight-line consumer can't reload an evicted
parameter bound to its incoming register where another operand of the
same node holds it (TIER1_ALLOCATION_CONSTRAINT, an internal failure):
such a function now goes through the CFG consumer, which moves values.

test-tier1-wide runs hand-written and random 128-bit IR and 300 of Tier
0's random 128-bit programs through the split by both paths, both
verifiers, the optimizer and both of Tier 1's paths with certificates,
natively, against the IR's interpreter and the programs' stack machine;
the split's refusals and its scan's twins. --tier1 --tier1-strict now
compiles every function of sqlite3.c (sqlite3Multiply128 and 160 too).

