From 93121b232ab1ea7b5c27430021bc52ad88d344a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 01:09:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/9] Tier 1: long double on the x87, in frame objects of
 values (#141)

F80 values no longer refuse a function: the CFG consumer keeps each one
in a 16-byte frame object of its own and computes on the x87 stack
between them, leaving it empty after each node, as Tier 0 does between
its slots. Selection's TIER1_RULE_X87 (its kind in the pattern's scale)
lists only the non-long-double operands as values (a load's or store's
address, a conversion's integer or floating operand), so no allocation
sees a long double; the plan marks the long double operands needed, and
tier1_emit_cfg_x87 reads them from their objects: constants as
immediates, loads and stores by fld and fstp, arithmetic by faddp and
its kin, fchs, comparisons by fucomip and setcc (parity for == and !=),
conversions with floats and doubles through an x87 scratch object, with
integers by fild and by fistp under a truncating control word (fisttp
is past the baseline), an unsigned 64-bit one's 2^63 adjustment as
Tier 0's. A long double phi is its edges' parallel copy through the x87
stack; the allocation places such edges (Tier1CfgInput.edge_copies),
and its verifier and the certificate take that placement. A call passes
a long double in its 16-byte stack slots and stores its st0 result into
its object; a return loads st0 before the epilogue.

test-tier1-wide runs 300 of Tier 0's random long double programs (calls
of C functions of long doubles, every conversion, NaNs, frame objects,
?:'s blocks) through the optimizer and both of Tier 1's paths, with
certificates, natively against their stack machine. The anchors that
used a long double for a refusal (the outcome test's mixed fixture and
the multicore driver test) now use a struct of one returned in st0,
which Tier 1 still refuses. c-testsuite's subset: Tier 1 compiles 428 of
its 430 functions (421 before).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01TiDLnvjnrjwbym9rZvTaVA
Signed-off-by: David Gonzalez Martin <davidgmbb@gmail.com>
