From a934e80867fdcc7b4516de417860de2ba02782e3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 02:09:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] Tier 1: aggregates the convention ignores (#141)

An empty struct, or one of unnamed bit-fields, is IGNORE: passed and
returned nowhere. Tier 1 now takes it:

- a call copies nothing for it;
- a parameter's copy is a frame object of its size, which the entry
  leaves as it is;
- neither a call nor a return copies an ignored result.

The lowering gave a call ABI records only when some argument's size
wasn't 0. A call whose only aggregates were empty structs, of no bytes,
had none, so Tier 1 placed its arguments by type. The empty struct's
address then took a register of its own, and every argument after it
moved one register along: f(e, 9) passed &e in rdi and 9 in rsi. That
miscompiled any Tier 1 caller of such a function. The lowering now
gives records to a call that passes an ignored aggregate too
(ir_typed_aggregate_call). Whether a function has one at all is found
once, by a scan of its arguments' locations, 8 a load
(ir_typed_ignored_arguments, with its scalar twin), so that no call of
a function without one reads its arguments' locations.

The rarer shapes' fixture gains empty and unnamed-bit-field structs,
passed and returned both ways between Tier 1's and clang's code; the
