From c27be7728cfef158d125d1c5f6ba01df7c8a934a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 03:12:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] Tier 1: the IR's vectors, in the vector class at their
 widths (#141)

Selection took no function with a vector. Now v4i32, v8i32 and v16i32
values within a function are Tier 1's: the vector class's, as floating
values are (xmm0 to xmm14, xmm15 its scratch), computed by AVX-512's
EVEX forms at their widths (TIER1_RULE_VECTOR, its kind in `scale`):

- constants: vpxord for zeros (and undef), vpternlogd 0xff for every
  bit, mov r11d and vpbroadcastd for a splat, and any other's words
  through the red zone into a vmovdqu32 (tier1_emit_cfg_vector_constant);
  a function with one keeps its frame out of the red zone;
- add, sub, mul, and, or and xor: vpaddd, vpsubd, vpmulld, vpandq,
  vporq and vpxord, three operands (unmasked, the quadword and and or
  are the doublewords' bits, and x86_semantics.c models those forms);
- not: vpternlogd 0x55; copy: vmovdqa64;
- vcmp: vpcmpd or vpcmpud into the mask's opmask register;
- vselect: vpblendmd under the mask;
- loads and stores: the floating rules and their address folds at the
  vector's width, vmovdqu32.

A function without vectors pays little of this per node: one
vectorized scan of it (ir_function_has_vectors_path) gates selection's
vector candidates, behind one test per node, the CFG consumer's class
and size columns and its chain check, and its red-zone flag reads the
constants' count.

The CFG consumer keeps a vector's slot at its width, aligned to 16 at
most, spills, reloads and moves it along edges at its width, and in a
function with vectors gives the class's call-save rows and temporary
slot the widest vector's bytes (Tier1EmitPrepared.vector_bytes).

A vector C passes or returns by value is an aggregate to the lowering,
in a vector register whole, which the rarer shapes' commit takes. Still
refused: an IR function whose own parameter or result is a vector value
(tier1_select_vector_signature), which C doesn't make, and a call with a
vector argument or result value.

test-tier1-masks gains five vector fixtures, run natively against the
IR's interpreter on both paths, with and without rematerialization:
every operation and predicate at each width, constants of each kind,
vector phis that swap through xmm15, 18 live zmm values spilled and
reloaded, and vectors saved across calls. test-checker-driver gains the
vectors' fixture, for x86-64-v4 and run where the host has AVX-512 F and
VL: vectors of each width passed and returned by value between Tier 1's
code and clang -O2's, both ways. tests/ir/vector-i32.c
compiles wholly by Tier 1 at x86-64-v4 and matches clang. sqlite3.c's
--tier1 object is byte-identical to the previous commit's by both
paths.

