From 9862b5638f0bb0b0d84cea18d5d4da01bd882822 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 10:14:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] Tier 1: one scan for long doubles per function (#141)

The long double commit tested every node of every function for an F80
(tier1_select_x87_node), in selection's candidates and the CFG
consumer's chain check, and looked for long double operands of each
return. On the frozen sqlite3.c, which has none, the test alone took
about 1.8M instructions of the --tier1 -c compile (an instruction
profile on the 9700X below perf's throttling rate). Selection now scans
the function's type column once (Tier1SelectPlan.long_doubles), 64
bytes a compare on the vector path, and the x87 tests run only where it
found one.

The scan is the wide split's I128 scan made general,
ir_function_has_type_path (ir.c), which the split now calls for I128.
test-tier1-wide checks its vector path against its scalar twin for both
types, on type columns of every length to 200 ending at a guarded page.

